<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Prut</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Prut River, Pruth</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Prut is a river of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a>, about 950 kilometres long, rising in the Ukrainian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carpathian-mountains">Carpathians</a> and flowing south-east to join the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> near Reni. For much of its course it forms the border between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romania">Romania</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moldova">Moldova</a>. In 1711 a Russian army under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> was surrounded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> on its banks, and from 1812 it marked the western boundary of Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/prut.jpg" alt="Prut" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Varga Attila · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prut_near_Hoverla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
